      // [src_fitzgerald_usc_screenplays, src_fitzgerald_usc_life, src_fitzgerald_princeton_gatsby | 1927-1938 | library page on the MGM archive + university-library biography + university-library curators] The MGM archive page quotes the screenwriter Budd Schulberg, his last collaborator on a film assignment, on Fitzgerald's determination to develop his screenwriting skills and his regard for film as an art form deserving as serious attention as his novels and plays, and records that for the Joan Crawford assignment he studied her films to tailor the role to her abilities and that his time on Three Comrades was logged in his own manuscript record; the biography records two unsuccessful Hollywood trips before the third produced a screen credit; Princeton notes that he went on marking changes in a personal copy of Gatsby after publication. Learning a new craft after earlier failures is specific behaviour, but the main witness is one admiring collaborator quoted on a page written to promote a library acquisition, and Mankiewicz's heavy rewriting of the final script shows the results were limited. SELF-CALIBRATION (agent): lowered from 68/0.54 to 62/0.48 and relabelled inference (admiring-source concentration: Schulberg's testimony and the 'seriousness' framing come from an acquisition page; interpretation stronger than behaviour).
      mastery_orientation: [62, 0.48, "i", "A"],
      // [src_fitzgerald_wilson1922, src_fitzgerald_princeton_gatsby, src_fitzgerald_usc_life, src_fitzgerald_yale_dimock | 1922-1925 | contemporary critic + university-library curators + university-library biography + university lecturer] The 1922 Bookman essay says that after This Side of Paradise he took up the ironical-pessimistic school of fiction and that the first version of The Beautiful and Damned ended in a run of catastrophes the critic thought arbitrary; Princeton's curators describe a first Gatsby set in the Midwest around 1885 with no Nick Carraway as narrator that he discarded for a version set in 1922; the USC biography records a stage play, The Vegetable, that failed at its 1923 tryout, and says Gatsby used a complex structure and a controlled narrative point of view; Yale's Wai Chee Dimock reads the novel as experimental counter-realism. Several documented changes of form and approach, but some did not succeed, the experimental reading is a later lecturer's and the first is a single contemporary account. SELF-CALIBRATION (agent): lowered from 66/0.54 to 62/0.50 (interpretation stronger than behaviour; retrospective reading).
      experimentation: [62, 0.5, "s", "A"],
      // [src_fitzgerald_britannica, src_fitzgerald_usc_life, src_fitzgerald_wilson1922, src_fitzgerald_princeton_gatsby | 1917-1925 | encyclopaedia biography + university-library biography + contemporary critic + university-library curators] Britannica (Arthur Mizener) calls This Side of Paradise a revelation of the new morality of the young and Gatsby the most profoundly American novel of its time; the USC biography says Scribner's first rejection praised his first novel's originality, that his early stories introduced a fresh character (the independent, determined young American woman) and that Gatsby marked a striking advance in technique; Princeton shows him discarding a first conception set in the Midwest around 1885 for one set in 1922. Against that, the 1922 Bookman essay called This Side of Paradise highly imitative and drawn from a bad model while granting a brilliant imagination whose next product could not be predicted. The praise is largely retrospective and from admiring biographers, and the one contemporary independent critic disputed the early originality, so inference-level at modest confidence. SELF-CALIBRATION (agent): lowered from 68/0.48 to 62/0.44 (retrospective praise; a contemporary critic's contrary view).
      creative_originality: [62, 0.44, "i", "A"],
