      experimentation: [74, 0.54, "s", "A"],
      // [src_harrison_rmg_story, src_harrison_rmg_h3, src_harrison_rmg_h4, src_harrison_rmg_attr, src_harrison_schaffer2014 | 1720s-1759 | museum story + museum records + museum page + university historian's essay] The museum says that in the mid-1720s the self-taught carpenter designed precision longcase clocks accurate to a second in a month, far better than any clocks of the time; that H1 was unaffected by a ship's motion because of its two interconnected balances; that H3 contained the bimetallic strip and the caged roller bearing, still used in thermostats and mechanical engineering; and that H4 used a radically new balance; its attraction page calls the timekeepers arguably the most important ever made, and Schaffer reports that they are now widely reckoned the most important ever constructed while warning against the lone-hero story and noting that the 2008-09 repair of H1 showed that parts of it were made by other skilled artisans. Specific new mechanisms are documented by the museum, but the praise is retrospective, no source read examines priority for the inventions, and part of the work was shared with others, so strong inference at a modest confidence. SELF-CALIBRATION (agent): lowered from 76/0.56 to 70/0.50 (retrospective praise and admiring-source concentration; interpretation stronger than behaviour, since the sources attribute some of the work to collaborators).
