      // [src_pauli_giulini, src_pauli_ndb | 1918-1957 | scholarly paper + biographical entry] The editors of his collected papers (as quoted by Giulini) say his work has one common denominator, a striving for symmetry and invariance, the invariants being for him 'the symbols of ultimate truth'; Giulini's survey lists relativity and Weyl's extension, the hydrogen atom in matrix mechanics, the exclusion principle, spin, the neutrino hypothesis, Kaluza-Klein theory, spin-statistics, the CPT theorem and parity among works resting on symmetry and group-theoretic ideas, and the NDB entry notes his positivist renunciation of untestable atomic models. A consistent, scholarly-documented pattern of working through general structural principles, but it rests chiefly on one survey and the editors' tribute, and it characterises a body of work rather than described conduct. SELF-CALIBRATION (agent): first-pass 72 / 0.52 lowered to 64 / 0.46 and relabelled inference for single-source dependence (Giulini and the editors he quotes) and interpretation stronger than behaviour.
      systems_abstraction: [64, 0.46, "i", "A"],
      // [src_pauli_aitchison, src_pauli_giulini | 1925-1957 | review + scholarly paper] Aitchison writes that Pauli's reaction to any new idea, even his own, was often sharply hostile but that he was always prepared to change his mind, and gives the case of Kronig's 1925 spin proposal, brushed aside and followed two years later by Pauli's own mathematical description of spin; Giulini records that on 17 January 1957 he wrote that he did not believe 'God is a weak left-hander' and was prepared to bet a high amount that the experiment would show mirror symmetry, that by 19 January he wrote to Wu that the asymmetry was 'empirically so well established', that on 27-28 January he told Weisskopf he was shocked and was already speculating how symmetry might be restored, and that in February 1957 he conceded Fierz's point after a week of sharp exchanges. Several documented reversals, but each came only after a firm initial rejection (and Giulini notes he had written down the parity-violating interaction in 1955 without believing it), and three of the four episodes come from one paper. SELF-CALIBRATION (agent): first-pass 66 / 0.54 lowered to 62 / 0.50 for single-source dependence (Giulini carries three episodes) and interpretation stronger than behaviour (the reversals are partial and 'always prepared to change his mind' is a reviewer's characterisation).
